{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\n`force_close_reason` is written when a close uses `--force=<reason>`. Nothing ever\nclears it. After `rebar reopen`, the value persists onto an `open` ticket, and a later\nclose that passes every gate and carries a valid completion signature leaves the\noriginal bypass text in place. The ticket then asserts two contradictory facts at once:\na signed completion-verifier PASS, and a recorded gate bypass.\n\nThe same applies to `close_class`, `close_reason`, and `completion_expectation`.\n\nAny audit keyed on `force_close_reason` — which is exactly how a project enforces that\nforce did not circumvent a gate, since a forced op records no certification — reads a\nproperly verified close as an unverified one. There is no supported way to clear it.\n\nReported against nava-rebar 0.13.1 (Python 3.14.7, macOS) by a client project.\n\n## Evidence\n\nReproduction, from a ticket whose commit carries a valid trailer:\n\n```\nrebar transition \"$T\" open closed --force=\"repro: bypassing the landing gate on purpose\"\nrebar reopen \"$T\"\nrebar list | python3 -c \"import sys,json;[print(t['status'], repr(t.get('force_close_reason'))) for t in json.load(sys.stdin) if t['ticket_id']=='$T']\"\n\ngit commit -q --amend -m \"Change feature.py\n\nrebar-ticket: $T\"\nrebar transition \"$T\" open closed\n```\n\nWhile the ticket is OPEN it still carries a force-close reason:\n\n```\nstatus            : open\nforce_close_reason: 'repro: bypassing the landing gate on purpose'\n```\n\nAfter a close that passed the landing check and the completion verifier with NO\noverride:\n\n```\nstatus            : closed\nclose_class       : None\nclose_reason      : None\nforce_close_reason: 'repro: bypassing the landing gate on purpose'\ncompletion-verifier: PASS\n```\n\n## Root cause\n\n`_fold_close_metadata` (`src/rebar/reducer/_processors_status.py`, ~line 69) applies four\nkeys under a present-only rule, and every branch only ever writes:\n\n```python\nif data.get(\"status\") == \"closed\" and data.get(\"force_close_reason\"):\n    state[\"force_close_reason\"] = data[\"force_close_reason\"]\n```\n\nTwo properties combine to produce the defect:\n\n1. The fold is gated on `status == \"closed\"`, so the `closed -> open` edge that `reopen`\n   emits never reaches it. `rebar reopen` is a plain alias for\n   `transition <id> closed open` (`src/rebar/_commands/transition.py`, ~line 772) and\n   carries no clearing step of its own.\n2. Because the rule is present-only, the clean close emits a STATUS event with no\n   `force_close_reason` key at all — and an absent key cannot clear a stale value.\n\nThe present-only rule is CORRECT for its stated purpose: stopping a losing concurrent\nclose from overwriting the fork winner's provenance. It was simply never extended to the\nreopen edge, where the value should be dropped rather than preserved. Any fix must keep\nthat concurrency guarantee intact.\n\n## Known affected data\n\nThe reporter states two tickets in their store were force-closed, then reopened,\nremediated, and closed through the plan-review and completion-verification gates with\nsigned attestations. Both still carry the original bypass text, including its\nnow-incorrect justification. Whether previously-reduced state needs a repair pass, or\nwhether re-reduction from the event log is sufficient once the fold is fixed, is part of\nthis ticket's scope to determine. Determined below under Plan step 2.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. Add `_clear_close_metadata(state, data)` in `src/rebar/reducer/_processors_status.py`,\n   modelled on the existing `_clear_claimed_session`, whose guard is\n   `data.get(\"current_status\") == \"in_progress\" and data.get(\"status\") != \"in_progress\"`.\n   The new guard is `data.get(\"current_status\") == \"closed\" and data.get(\"status\") != \"closed\"`;\n   it pops `close_class`, `close_reason`, `force_close_reason`, `completion_expectation`.\n   `current_status` is the STATUS event's expected-prior-status field that `process_status`\n   already reads for fork detection (line 79). Call it at the two winner sites next to\n   `_clear_claimed_session`; the losing-fork `else` branch never calls it.\n   `_fold_close_metadata` itself stays present-only and unchanged.\n2. Existing stores have two persisted reduced forms, handled differently:\n   - The per-ticket reducer cache, keyed on `_REDUCER_CACHE_VERSION` in\n     `src/rebar/reducer/_cache.py` (\"Increment it whenever projection semantics change\").\n     Bump it 7 -> 8 so every cached state is recomputed from the events after the latest\n     SNAPSHOT. For a ticket with no SNAPSHOT, that is the whole log, so the stale keys clear.\n   - SNAPSHOT `compiled_state`. `process_snapshot` copies it verbatim and replay skips the\n     retired source events, so a SNAPSHOT taken after a forced close + reopen keeps the\n     stale keys and this fold change cannot reach them. Worked example from this repo's\n     tracker: aabf-d89e's SNAPSHOT is `status: closed` with `force_close_reason` and\n     `last_reopened_at` set, while its last closing STATUS event has no force reason; 8\n     such closed SNAPSHOTs and 4 open/in_progress ones exist. Repairing them requires\n     rewriting SNAPSHOTs in the shared tracker (`rebuild_snapshot_from_full_log`), a\n     store mutation with a clean scope boundary, so it is split to bug\n     sugarcandy-fusible-hogget (df72-d570-8c77-44d4, linked discovered_from). This ticket\n     does NOT claim to repair compacted tickets.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] Force-close (`open -> closed` with `force_close_reason=\"bypass\"`, `close_class`,\n  `close_reason`, `completion_expectation` all set), then reopen (`closed -> open`): the\n  reduced state has none of the 4 keys. Asserted in `tests/unit/test_reducer_status.py`.\n- [x] A later `open -> closed` with no close metadata yields `status == \"closed\"` and no\n  `force_close_reason` key. Asserted end to end through the library (`rebar.transition`\n  on a temp store, `force=` then `reopen`, then a clean close) in the same file.\n- [x] Deleting the new clearing call makes both tests above fail (observed red before the\n  fix; recorded in the commit message).\n- [x] Concurrency: a losing fork event `closed -> open` (larger UUID than the winner)\n  leaves the winner's `force_close_reason` in place, and a losing `* -> closed` fork does\n  not overwrite the winner's close metadata. Asserted in the new file; the existing\n  `test_fold_close_metadata_*` tests in `tests/unit/test_administrative_close_dispositions.py`\n  and `test_status_fold_replay_equivalence` in `tests/unit/test_processors_status_split.py`\n  pass unchanged.\n- [x] A reducer cache entry written under version 7 holding a stale `force_close_reason`\n  for an uncompacted (no SNAPSHOT) ticket is ignored after the bump: reducing that ticket\n  dir returns state without the key. Asserted in the new file.\n- [x] SNAPSHOT-carried stale keys are explicitly out of scope and tracked in\n  sugarcandy-fusible-hogget; that link exists before this ticket closes.\n- [x] The event log still contains the forced STATUS event with its `force_close_reason`\n  after reopen: reading the ticket dir's STATUS event files back shows it.\n\n## Testing\n\n`tests/unit/test_reducer_status.py` does not exist yet; this ticket creates it.\n\n```\n.venv/bin/pytest tests/unit/test_reducer_status.py tests/unit/test_administrative_close_dispositions.py tests/unit/test_processors_status_split.py -p no:randomly -q\nmake lint && make typecheck && make verify\n```\n"}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1792369750243652001,"uuid":"3611fb5e-9d43-4cfa-9891-0dcff8762352"}